</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Collision Avoidance Force Estimation</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, the decision variables are control points <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> of a B-spline curve.
Each <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> possesses its own environment information independently.
Initially, a naive B-spline curve <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying terminal constraints is given, regardless of collision.
Then, the optimization procedure starts.
For each colliding segment detected in an iteration, a collision-free path <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated.
Each control point <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of the colliding segment, after that, will be assigned an anchor point <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> at the obstacle surface with a corresponding repulsive direction vector <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, as shown in Fig.<a href="#S2.F3.sf1" title="In Fig. 3 ‣ II-B Euclidean Signed Distance Field (ESDF) ‣ II Related Work ‣ EGO-Planner: An ESDF-free Gradient-based Local Planner for Quadrotors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a>.
Denote by <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathbb{N}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathbb{N}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> the index of control points, and <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> the index of <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{p},\mathbf{v}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{p},\mathbf{v}\}</annotation></semantics></math> pair.
Note that each <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{p},\mathbf{v}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{p},\mathbf{v}\}</annotation></semantics></math> pair only belongs to one specific control point. For brevity, we omit the subscript <math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ij" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ij</annotation></semantics></math> without causing ambiguity.
The detailed <math id="S3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{p},\mathbf{v}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{p},\mathbf{v}\}</annotation></semantics></math> pair generation procedure in this paper is summarized in Alg.<a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ II-B Euclidean Signed Distance Field (ESDF) ‣ II Related Work ‣ EGO-Planner: An ESDF-free Gradient-based Local Planner for Quadrotors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and is illustrated in Fig.<a href="#S2.F3.sf2" title="In Fig. 3 ‣ II-B Euclidean Signed Distance Field (ESDF) ‣ II Related Work ‣ EGO-Planner: An ESDF-free Gradient-based Local Planner for Quadrotors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(b)</span></a>.
Then the obstacle distance from <math id="S3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to the <math id="S3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> obstacle is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{ij}=(\mathbf{Q}_{i}-\mathbf{p}_{ij})\cdot\mathbf{v}_{ij}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{ij}=(\mathbf{Q}_{i}-\mathbf{p}_{ij})\cdot\mathbf{v}_{ij}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In order to avoid duplicative <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{p},\mathbf{v}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{p},\mathbf{v}\}</annotation></semantics></math> pair generation before the trajectory escapes from the current obstacle during the first several iterations, we adopt a criterion that considers an obstacle which the control point <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in as newly discovered, only if the current <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{ij}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{ij}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for all valid <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.
Besides, this criterion allows only necessary obstacles that contribute to the final trajectory to be taken into optimization.
Thus, the operation time is significantly reduced.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To incorporate necessary environmental awareness into the local planner, we need to explicitly construct an objective function that keeps the trajectory away from obstacles. ESDF provides this vital collision information but with the price of a heavy computation burden.
In addition, as shown in Fig.<a href="#S1.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ I Introduction ‣ EGO-Planner: An ESDF-free Gradient-based Local Planner for Quadrotors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, ESDF-based planners can easily fall into a local minimum and fail to escape from obstacles, due to the insufficient or even wrong information from ESDF.
To avoid such situations, an additional front-end is always needed to provide a collision-free initial trajectory.
The above methodology outperforms ESDF in providing the vital information for collision avoidance, since the explicitly designed repulsive force can be fairly effective regarding various missions and environments.
Moreover, the proposed method has no requirement for collision-free initialization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gradient-Based Trajectory Optimization</span></h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Problem Formulation</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, the trajectory is parameterized by a uniform B-spline curve <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is uniquely determined by its degree <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> control points <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\mathbf{Q}_{1},\mathbf{Q}_{2},\cdots,\mathbf{Q}_{N_{c}}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\mathbf{Q}_{1},\mathbf{Q}_{2},\cdots,\mathbf{Q}_{N_{c}}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a knot vector <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{t_{1},t_{2},\cdots,t_{M}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{t_{1},t_{2},\cdots,t_{M}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{m}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{m}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=N_{c}+p_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=N_{c}+p_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For simplicity and efficiency of trajectory evaluation, the B-spline used in our method is uniform, which means each knot is separated by the same time interval <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle t=t_{m+1}-t_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle t=t_{m+1}-t_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> from its predecessor.
The problem formulation in this paper is based on the current state-of-the-art quadrotor local planning framework Fast-Planner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2008.08835v2/cvx_hull_prot.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/250;" width="429" height="250"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Convex hull property of the B-spline curve. Gray points represent control points. The whole curve stays inside the feasibility bounding box(black dotted box) as long as all the control points are in that box. Without loss of generality, each convex hull consists of four vertexes. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">B-spline enjoys convex hull property.
This property indicates that a single span of a B-spline curve is merely controlled by <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{b}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{b}+1</annotation></semantics></math> successive control points and lies within the convex hull of these points.
For example, a span within <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{i},t_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{i},t_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math> lies inside the convex hull formed by <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{Q}_{i-p_{b}},\mathbf{Q}_{i-p_{b}+1},\cdots,\mathbf{Q}_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{Q}_{i-p_{b}},\mathbf{Q}_{i-p_{b}+1},\cdots,\mathbf{Q}_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Another property is that the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> derivative of a B-spline is still a B-spline with order <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{b,k}=p_{b}-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{b,k}=p_{b}-k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle t</annotation></semantics></math> is identical alone <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, the control points of the velocity <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, acceleration <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and jerk <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> curves are obtained by</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{i}=\frac{\mathbf{Q}_{i+1}-\mathbf{Q}_{i}}{\triangle t},\ \mathbf{A}_{i}=\frac{\mathbf{V}_{i+1}-\mathbf{V}_{i}}{\triangle t},\ \mathbf{J}_{i}=\frac{\mathbf{A}_{i+1}-\mathbf{A}_{i}}{\triangle t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{i}=\frac{\mathbf{Q}_{i+1}-\mathbf{Q}_{i}}{\triangle t},\ \mathbf{A}_{i}=\frac{\mathbf{V}_{i+1}-\mathbf{V}_{i}}{\triangle t},\ \mathbf{J}_{i}=\frac{\mathbf{A}_{i+1}-\mathbf{A}_{i}}{\triangle t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We follow the work of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> to plan the control points <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> in a reduced space of <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">differentially flat outputs</span>.
The optimization problem is then formulated as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{min}_{\mathbf{Q}}J=\lambda_{s}J_{s}+\lambda_{c}J_{c}+\lambda_{d}J_{d}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>𝐐</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{min}_{\mathbf{Q}}J=\lambda_{s}J_{s}+\lambda_{c}J_{c}+\lambda_{d}J_{d},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is the smoothness penalty, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is for collision, and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates feasibility.
<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{s},\lambda_{c},\lambda_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{s},\lambda_{c},\lambda_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are weights for each penalty terms.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span>1 </span>Smoothness penalty</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, the smoothness penalty is formulized as the time integral over square derivatives of the trajectory (acceleration, jerk, etc.).
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, only geometric information of the trajectory is taken regardless of time allocation.
In this paper, we combine both methods to penalize squared acceleration and jerk without time integration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Benefiting from the convex hull property, minimizing the control points of second and third order derivatives of the B-spline trajectory is sufficient to reduce these derivatives along the whole curve.
Therefore, the smoothness penalty function is formulated as</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{s}=\sum_{i=1}^{N_{c}-1}\|\mathbf{A}_{i}\|_{2}^{2}+\sum_{i=1}^{N_{c}-2}\|\mathbf{J}_{i}\|_{2}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{s}=\sum_{i=1}^{N_{c}-1}\|\mathbf{A}_{i}\|_{2}^{2}+\sum_{i=1}^{N_{c}-2}\|\mathbf{J}_{i}\|_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which minimizes high order derivatives, making the whole trajectory smooth.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span>2 </span>Collision penalty</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Collision penalty pushes control points away from obstacles.
This is achieved by adopting a safety clearance <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> and punishing control points with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{ij}&lt;s_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{ij}&lt;s_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In order to further facilitate optimization, we construct a twice continuously differentiable penalty function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and suppress its slope as <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> decreases, which yields the piecewise function</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle j_{c}(i,j)=\left\{\begin{aligned} &amp;0&amp;(c_{ij}\leq 0)\\
&amp;c_{ij}^{3}&amp;(0&lt;c_{ij}\leq s_{f})\\
&amp;3s_{f}c_{ij}^{2}-3s_{f}^{2}c_{ij}+s_{f}^{3}&amp;(c_{ij}&gt;s_{f})\end{aligned}\right.," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle j_{c}(i,j)=\left\{\begin{aligned} &amp;0&amp;(c_{ij}\leq 0)\\
&amp;c_{ij}^{3}&amp;(0&lt;c_{ij}\leq s_{f})\\
&amp;3s_{f}c_{ij}^{2}-3s_{f}^{2}c_{ij}+s_{f}^{3}&amp;(c_{ij}&gt;s_{f})\end{aligned}\right.,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle c_{ij}=s_{f}-d_{ij},\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\quad" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle c_{ij}=s_{f}-d_{ij},\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{c}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{c}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is the cost value produced by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{p},\mathbf{v}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{p},\mathbf{v}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> pairs on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The cost on each <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is evaluated independently and accumulated from all corresponding <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{p},\mathbf{v}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{p},\mathbf{v}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> pairs.
Thus, a control point obtains a higher trajectory deformation weight if it discovers more obstacles.
Specifically, the cost value added to the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> control point is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{c}(\mathbf{Q}_{i})=\sum_{j=1}^{N_{p}}j_{c}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{c}(\mathbf{Q}_{i})=\sum_{j=1}^{N_{p}}j_{c}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{p},\mathbf{v}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{p},\mathbf{v}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> pairs belonging to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Combining costs on all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> yields the total cost <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{c}=\sum_{i=1}^{N_{c}}j_{c}(\mathbf{Q}_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{c}=\sum_{i=1}^{N_{c}}j_{c}(\mathbf{Q}_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike traditional ESDF-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, which compute gradient by trilinear interpolation on the field, we obtain gradient by directly computing the derivative of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, which gives</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{\partial J_{c}}{\partial\mathbf{Q}_{i}}=\sum_{i=1}^{N_{c}}\sum_{j=1}^{N_{p}}\mathbf{v}_{ij}\left\{\begin{aligned} &amp;0&amp;(c_{ij}\leq 0)\\
&amp;-3c_{ij}^{2}&amp;(0&lt;c_{ij}\leq s_{f})\\
&amp;-6s_{f}c_{ij}+3s_{f}^{2}&amp;(c_{ij}&gt;s_{f})\end{aligned}\right.." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></munderover><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial J_{c}}{\partial\mathbf{Q}_{i}}=\sum_{i=1}^{N_{c}}\sum_{j=1}^{N_{p}}\mathbf{v}_{ij}\left\{\begin{aligned} &amp;0&amp;(c_{ij}\leq 0)\\
&amp;-3c_{ij}^{2}&amp;(0&lt;c_{ij}\leq s_{f})\\
&amp;-6s_{f}c_{ij}+3s_{f}^{2}&amp;(c_{ij}&gt;s_{f})\end{aligned}\right..</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span>3 </span>Feasibility penalty</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Feasibility is ensured by restricting the higher order derivatives of the trajectory on every single dimension, i.e., applying <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathbf{\Phi}^{(k)}_{r}(t)|&lt;\mathbf{\Phi}^{(k)}_{r,max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msubsup><mi>𝚽</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathbf{\Phi}^{(k)}_{r}(t)|&lt;\mathbf{\Phi}^{(k)}_{r,max}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\{x,y,z\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\{x,y,z\}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates each dimension.
Thanks to the convex hull property, constraining derivatives of the control points is sufficient for constraining the whole B-spline.
Therefore, the penalty function is formulated as</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{d}=\sum_{i=1}^{N_{c}}w_{v}F(\mathbf{V}_{i})+\sum_{i=1}^{N_{c}-1}w_{a}F(\mathbf{A}_{i})+\sum_{i=1}^{N_{c}-2}w_{j}F(\mathbf{J}_{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{d}=\sum_{i=1}^{N_{c}}w_{v}F(\mathbf{V}_{i})+\sum_{i=1}^{N_{c}-1}w_{a}F(\mathbf{A}_{i})+\sum_{i=1}^{N_{c}-2}w_{j}F(\mathbf{J}_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{v},w_{a},w_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{v},w_{a},w_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are weights for each terms and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a twice continuously differentiable metric function of higher order derivatives of control points.</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\mathbf{C})=\mathop{\sum}_{r=x,y,z}f(c_{r})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\mathbf{C})=\mathop{\sum}_{r=x,y,z}f(c_{r}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f(c_{r})=\left\{\begin{aligned} &amp;a_{1}c_{r}^{2}+b_{1}c_{r}+c_{1}&amp;(c_{r}\leq-c_{j})\\
&amp;(-\lambda c_{m}-c_{r})^{3}&amp;(-c_{j}&lt;c_{r}&lt;-\lambda c_{m})\\
&amp;0&amp;(-\lambda c_{m}\leq c_{r}\leq\lambda c_{m})\\
&amp;(c_{r}-\lambda c_{m})^{3}&amp;(\lambda c_{m}&lt;c_{r}&lt;c_{j})\\
&amp;a_{2}c_{r}^{2}+b_{2}c_{r}+c_{2}&amp;(c_{r}\geq c_{j})\end{aligned}\right.," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(c_{r})=\left\{\begin{aligned} &amp;a_{1}c_{r}^{2}+b_{1}c_{r}+c_{1}&amp;(c_{r}\leq-c_{j})\\
&amp;(-\lambda c_{m}-c_{r})^{3}&amp;(-c_{j}&lt;c_{r}&lt;-\lambda c_{m})\\
&amp;0&amp;(-\lambda c_{m}\leq c_{r}\leq\lambda c_{m})\\
&amp;(c_{r}-\lambda c_{m})^{3}&amp;(\lambda c_{m}&lt;c_{r}&lt;c_{j})\\
&amp;a_{2}c_{r}^{2}+b_{2}c_{r}+c_{2}&amp;(c_{r}\geq c_{j})\end{aligned}\right.,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{r}\in\mathbf{C}\in\{\mathbf{V}_{i},\mathbf{A}_{i},\mathbf{J}_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{r}\in\mathbf{C}\in\{\mathbf{V}_{i},\mathbf{A}_{i},\mathbf{J}_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},b_{1},c_{1},a_{2},b_{2},c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},b_{1},c_{1},a_{2},b_{2},c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are chosen to meet the second-order continuity, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is the derivative limit, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the splitting points of the quadratic interval and the cubic interval.
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&lt;1-\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&lt;1-\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is an elastic coefficient with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\ll 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\ll 1</annotation></semantics></math> to make the final results meet the constraints, since the cost function is a tradeoff of all weighted terms.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Numerical Optimization</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The formulated problem in this paper features in two aspects.
Firstly, the objective function <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> alters adaptively according to the newly found obstacles.
It requires the solver to be able to restart fast.
Secondly, quadratic terms dominate the formulation of the objective function, making <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> approximate quadratic.
It means that the utilization of Hessian information can significantly accelerate the convergence.
However, obtaining the exact inverse Hessian is prohibitive in real-time applications since it consumes nonnegligible massive computation.
To circumvent this, quasi-Newton methods that approximate the inverse Hessian from gradient information are adopted.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since the performance of a solver is problem dependent, we compare three algorithms belonging to quasi-Newton methods.
They are Barzilai-Borwein method<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> which is capable of fast restart with most crude Hessian estimation, truncated Newton method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> which estimates Hessian by adding multiple tiny perturbations to a given state, L-BFGS method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> which approximates Hessian from previous objective function evaluations but requires a serial of iterations to reach a relatively accurate estimation.
Comparison in Sec.<a href="#S6.SS2" title="VI-B Optimization Algorithms Comparison ‣ VI Experiment Results ‣ EGO-Planner: An ESDF-free Gradient-based Local Planner for Quadrotors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI-B</span></a> states that L-BFGS outperforms the other two algorithms with appropriately selected memory size, balancing the loss of restart and the accuracy of inverse Hessian estimation.
This algorithm is briefly explained as follows.
For an unconstrained optimization problem <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{min}_{\mathbf{x}\in{\mathbb{R}^{n}}}f(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{min}_{\mathbf{x}\in{\mathbb{R}^{n}}}f(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, the updating for <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> follows the approximated Newton step</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{k+1}=\mathbf{x}_{k}-\alpha_{k}\mathbf{H}_{k}\nabla\mathbf{f}_{k}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>𝐇</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{k+1}=\mathbf{x}_{k}-\alpha_{k}\mathbf{H}_{k}\nabla\mathbf{f}_{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the step length and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is updated at every iteration by means of the formula</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{k+1}=\mathbf{V}_{k}^{T}\mathbf{H}_{k}\mathbf{V}_{k}+\rho_{k}\mathbf{s}_{k}\mathbf{s}_{k}^{T}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{k+1}=\mathbf{V}_{k}^{T}\mathbf{H}_{k}\mathbf{V}_{k}+\rho_{k}\mathbf{s}_{k}\mathbf{s}_{k}^{T},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}=(\mathbf{y}_{k}^{T}\mathbf{s}_{k})^{-1},\mathbf{V}_{k}=\mathbf{I}-\rho_{k}\mathbf{y}_{k}\mathbf{s}_{k}^{T},\mathbf{s}_{k}=\mathbf{x}_{k+1}-\mathbf{x}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}=(\mathbf{y}_{k}^{T}\mathbf{s}_{k})^{-1},\mathbf{V}_{k}=\mathbf{I}-\rho_{k}\mathbf{y}_{k}\mathbf{s}_{k}^{T},\mathbf{s}_{k}=\mathbf{x}_{k+1}-\mathbf{x}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{k}=\nabla\mathbf{f}_{k+1}-\nabla\mathbf{f}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{k}=\nabla\mathbf{f}_{k+1}-\nabla\mathbf{f}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is not calculated explicitly.
The algorithm right multiplies <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\mathbf{f}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\mathbf{f}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to Equ.<a href="#S4.E12" title="In IV-B Numerical Optimization ‣ IV Gradient-Based Trajectory Optimization ‣ EGO-Planner: An ESDF-free Gradient-based Local Planner for Quadrotors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> and recursively expands for <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> steps and then yields the efficient two-loop recursion updating method<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, resulting in linear time/space complexity.
The weight of Barzilai-Borwein step is used as the initial inverse Hessian <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{k}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{k}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for L-BFGS updating, which is</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{k}^{0}=\frac{\mathbf{s}_{k-1}^{T}\mathbf{y}_{k-1}}{\mathbf{y}_{k-1}^{T}\mathbf{y}_{k-1}}\mathbf{I}\ or\ \frac{\mathbf{s}_{k-1}^{T}\mathbf{s}_{k-1}}{\mathbf{s}_{k-1}^{T}\mathbf{y}_{k-1}}\mathbf{I}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{k}^{0}=\frac{\mathbf{s}_{k-1}^{T}\mathbf{y}_{k-1}}{\mathbf{y}_{k-1}^{T}\mathbf{y}_{k-1}}\mathbf{I}\ or\ \frac{\mathbf{s}_{k-1}^{T}\mathbf{s}_{k-1}}{\mathbf{s}_{k-1}^{T}\mathbf{y}_{k-1}}\mathbf{I}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">A monotone line search under strong Wolfe condition is used to enforce convergence.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Time Re-allocation and Trajectory Refinement</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Allocating an accurate time profile before the optimization is unreasonable, since the planner knows no information about the final trajectory then.
Therefore, an additional time re-allocation procedure is vital to ensure dynamical feasibility.
Previous works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> parameterize the trajectory as a non-uniform B-spline and iteratively lengthen a subset of knot spans when some segments exceed derivative limits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, one knot span <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle t_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle t_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> influences multiple control points and vice versa, leading to high-order discontinuity to the previous trajectory when adjusting knot spans near the start state.
In this section, a uniform B-spline trajectory <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is re-generated with reasonable time re-allocation according to the safe trajectory <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> from <a href="#S4" title="IV Gradient-Based Trajectory Optimization ‣ EGO-Planner: An ESDF-free Gradient-based Local Planner for Quadrotors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>.
Then, an anisotropic curve fitting method is proposed to make <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> freely optimize its control points to meet higher order derivative constraints while maintaining a nearly identical shape to <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2008.08835v2/fitting.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/151;" width="476" height="151"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Optimizing trajectory <math id="S5.F5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> to fit trajectory <math id="S5.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> while adjusting smoothness and feasibility. Black and green dots are sample points on the trajectory. The displacement between <math id="S5.F5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}(\alpha T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}(\alpha T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.F5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}(\alpha T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}(\alpha T)</annotation></semantics></math> breaks down into <math id="S5.F5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.F5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> along two ellipse principal axes. Points at the red ellipse surface produce identical penalties. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Firstly, as Fast-Planner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> does, we compute the limits exceeding ratio,</p>
<table id="S5.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{e}=max\{|\mathbf{V}_{i,r}/v_{m}|,\sqrt{|\mathbf{A}_{j,r}/a_{m}|},\sqrt[3]{|\mathbf{J}_{k,r}/j_{m}|},1\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo>,</mo><mroot><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></mroot><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{e}=max\{|\mathbf{V}_{i,r}/v_{m}|,\sqrt{|\mathbf{A}_{j,r}/a_{m}|},\sqrt[3]{|\mathbf{J}_{k,r}/j_{m}|},1\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\cdots,N_{c}-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\cdots,N_{c}-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\{1,\cdots,N_{c}-2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\{1,\cdots,N_{c}-2\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,\cdots,N_{c}-3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,\cdots,N_{c}-3\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\{x,y,z\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\{x,y,z\}</annotation></semantics></math> axis.
A notion with subscript <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> represents the limitation of a derivative.
<math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates how much we should lengthen the time allocation for <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> relative to <math id="S5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that <math id="S5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are inversely proportional to <math id="S5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle t</annotation></semantics></math>, the square of <math id="S5.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle t</annotation></semantics></math> and the cubic of <math id="S5.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle t</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, from Equ.<a href="#S4.E2" title="In IV-A Problem Formulation ‣ IV Gradient-Based Trajectory Optimization ‣ EGO-Planner: An ESDF-free Gradient-based Local Planner for Quadrotors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
Then we obtain the new time span of <math id="S5.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S5.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle t^{\prime}=r_{e}\triangle t." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle t^{\prime}=r_{e}\triangle t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> of time span <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is initially generated under boundary constraints while maintaining the identical shape and control points number to <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, by solving a closed-form min-least square problem.
The smoothness and feasibility are then refined by optimization.
The penalty function <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> formulated by linear combinations of smoothness (Sec.<a href="#S4.SS1.SSS1" title="IV-A1 Smoothness penalty ‣ IV-A Problem Formulation ‣ IV Gradient-Based Trajectory Optimization ‣ EGO-Planner: An ESDF-free Gradient-based Local Planner for Quadrotors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A1</span></a>), feasibility (Sec.<a href="#S4.SS1.SSS3" title="IV-A3 Feasibility penalty ‣ IV-A Problem Formulation ‣ IV Gradient-Based Trajectory Optimization ‣ EGO-Planner: An ESDF-free Gradient-based Local Planner for Quadrotors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A3</span></a>) and curve fitting (introduced later) is</p>
<table id="S5.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{min}_{\mathbf{Q}}J^{\prime}=\lambda_{s}J_{s}+\lambda_{d}J_{d}+\lambda_{f}J_{f}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>𝐐</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{min}_{\mathbf{Q}}J^{\prime}=\lambda_{s}J_{s}+\lambda_{d}J_{d}+\lambda_{f}J_{f},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is the weight of fitness term.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The fitting penalty function <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is formulated as the integral of anisotropic displacements from points <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}(\alpha T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}(\alpha T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> to the corresponding <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}(\alpha T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}(\alpha T)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are the trajectory duration of <math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the fitted curve <math id="S5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is already collision-free, we assign the axial displacement of two curves with low penalty weight to relax smoothness adjustment restriction, and radial displacement with high penalty weight to avoid collision.
To achieve this, we use the spheroidal metric, shown in Fig.<a href="#S5.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ V Time Re-allocation and Trajectory Refinement ‣ EGO-Planner: An ESDF-free Gradient-based Local Planner for Quadrotors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, such that displacements at the same spheroid surface produce identical penalties.
The spheroid we use for <math id="S5.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}(\alpha T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}(\alpha T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by rotating an ellipse centering at <math id="S5.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}(\alpha T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}(\alpha T)</annotation></semantics></math> about one of its principal axes, the tangent line <math id="S5.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\mathbf{\Phi}}_{s}(\alpha T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\mathbf{\Phi}}_{s}(\alpha T)</annotation></semantics></math>.
So the axial displacement <math id="S5.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and radial displacement <math id="S5.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> can be calculated by</p>
<table id="S5.E17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S5.E17X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E17X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d_{a}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E17X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left(\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}-\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}\right)\cdot\frac{\dot{\mathbf{\Phi}}_{s}}{\|\dot{\mathbf{\Phi}}_{s}\|}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left(\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}-\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}\right)\cdot\frac{\dot{\mathbf{\Phi}}_{s}}{\|\dot{\mathbf{\Phi}}_{s}\|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.E17Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E17Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E17Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left\|(\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}-\mathbf{\Phi}_{s})\times\frac{\dot{\mathbf{\Phi}}_{s}}{\|\dot{\mathbf{\Phi}}_{s}\|}\right\|." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left\|(\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}-\mathbf{\Phi}_{s})\times\frac{\dot{\mathbf{\Phi}}_{s}}{\|\dot{\mathbf{\Phi}}_{s}\|}\right\|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The fitness penalty function is</p>
<table id="S5.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{f}=\int_{0}^{1}\left[\frac{d_{a}(\alpha T^{\prime})^{2}}{a^{2}}+\frac{d_{r}(\alpha T^{\prime})^{2}}{b^{2}}\right]\mathrm{d}\alpha," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{f}=\int_{0}^{1}\left[\frac{d_{a}(\alpha T^{\prime})^{2}}{a^{2}}+\frac{d_{r}(\alpha T^{\prime})^{2}}{b^{2}}\right]\mathrm{d}\alpha,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> are semi-major and semi-minor axis of the ellipse, respectively.
The problem is solved by L-BFGS.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiment Results</span></h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span> </span><span id="S6.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Implementation Details</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The planning framework is summarized in Alg.<a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ VI-A Implementation Details ‣ VI Experiment Results ‣ EGO-Planner: An ESDF-free Gradient-based Local Planner for Quadrotors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
We set the B-spline order as <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{b}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{b}=3</annotation></semantics></math>.
The number of control points <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> alters around 25, which is determined by the planning horizon (about 7m) and the initial distance interval (about 0.3m) of adjacent points.
These are empirical parameters that balance the complexity of the problem with degrees of freedom.
The time complexity is <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N_{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N_{c})</annotation></semantics></math>, since one control point only affects nearby segments according to the local support property of B-spline.
The complexity of L-BFGS is also linear on the same relative tolerance.
For collision-free path searching, we adopt A*, which has a good advantage that the path <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> always tends to be close to the obstacle surface naturally.
Therefore, we can directly select <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> without obstacle surface searching.
For vector <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in Fig.<a href="#S2.F3.sf2" title="In Fig. 3 ‣ II-B Euclidean Signed Distance Field (ESDF) ‣ II Related Work ‣ EGO-Planner: An ESDF-free Gradient-based Local Planner for Quadrotors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(b)</span></a>, it can be deduced by the property of uniform B-spline parameterization, that the <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</p>
<table id="S6.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}_{i}=\frac{\mathbf{Q}_{i+1}-\mathbf{Q}_{i-1}}{2\triangle t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}_{i}=\frac{\mathbf{Q}_{i+1}-\mathbf{Q}_{i-1}}{2\triangle t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which can be efficiently computed.
Equ.<a href="#S5.E18" title="In V Time Re-allocation and Trajectory Refinement ‣ EGO-Planner: An ESDF-free Gradient-based Local Planner for Quadrotors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> is discretized to a finite number of points <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}(k\triangle t^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{f}(k\triangle t^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}(k\triangle t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}_{s}(k\triangle t)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{N},0\leq k\leq\lfloor{T/\triangle t}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{N},0\leq k\leq\lfloor{T/\triangle t}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>.
To further enforce safety, a collision check of a circular pipe with a fixed radius around the final trajectory is performed to provide enough obstacle clearance.
The optimizer stops when no collision is detected.
Real-world experiments are presented on the same flight platform of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> with depth acquired by Intel RealSense D435<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            https://www.intelrealsense.com/depth-camera-d435/</span></span></span>.
Furthermore, we modify the ROS driver of Intel RealSense to enable the laser emitter strobe every other frame.
This allows the device to output high quality depth images with the help of the emitter, and along with binocular images free from laser interference.
The modified driver is open-sourced as well.</p>
</div>
